---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import ProvingGroundHome from "../../components/proving-ground/ProvingGroundHome";

export const prerender = true;

export const title = "The Proving Ground";
export const navCategory = "proving-ground";
---

<Layout title="The Proving Ground - Matrix Hub">
  <div class="container mx-auto px-4 py-8">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4 text-green-400">
      🏟️ The Proving Ground
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 mb-4">
      MTX 101 teaches the skills in words. The Proving Ground is where you{' '}
      <strong class="text-gray-100">do them</strong> — hands-on training for
      checking a token contract yourself, built from Matrix Hub's own real
      contracts and its own real fake.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6">
      Three short modules: run the 30-second address check against
      lookalikes until it is reflex, watch a direct mint happen step by step,
      and read a verified contract on PolygonScan like a pro. No lectures —
      you learn by doing, and the ground keeps your score.
    </p>

    <!-- Honest labelling, up front: training only. -->
    <div
      class="bg-green-900/40 border-2 border-green-500 rounded-lg p-4 mb-8 text-green-100"
    >
      <strong>Training, stated plainly:</strong> nothing here connects a
      wallet, moves money, or asks you to sign anything. The mint module is a
      labelled simulation; the only live data on these pages is read-only
      (the mint rate, read fresh from the contract). Your progress is stored
      only in this browser, on this device.
    </div>

    <ProvingGroundHome client:only="react" />

    <div class="mt-12 bg-gray-800 border border-gray-600 rounded-lg p-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-400 mb-3">
        Where the facts come from
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-3">
        Every address, figure, and warning in these modules comes from the
        same public proof the{' '}
        <a href="/security" class="text-green-400 hover:underline">
          /security
        </a>{' '}
        page publishes — the real MTX contract, the flagged fake, the supply
        and cap, and who controls the contracts, all checkable on PolygonScan.
        MTX is the utility token of the Matrix Hub ecosystem; nothing here is
        financial advice, and nothing here promises anything about price.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-300">
        New to the series? The MTX 101 posts run daily on our{' '}
        <a
          href="https://t.me/matrixhuborg"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="text-green-400 hover:underline"
        >
          Telegram channel
        </a>{' '}
        — the Proving Ground is their practice field.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Built from the commons: the open-source projects this section is
         actually built from — named, licensed, linked. -->
    <div class="mt-8 bg-gray-800 border border-gray-600 rounded-lg p-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-green-400 mb-3">
        Built from the commons
      </h2>
      <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">
        This section stands on open-source software, the way the whole site
        does. These are the projects it is actually built from — thank you to
        everyone who ships their work for others to build on:
      </p>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-300">
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/withastro/astro"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-green-400 hover:underline font-bold"
          >
            Astro
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-gray-400">(MIT)</span> — the static site framework
          these pages are built and shipped with.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/facebook/react"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-green-400 hover:underline font-bold"
          >
            React
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-gray-400">(MIT)</span> — the interactive islands
          the three training modules run as.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/ethers-io/ethers.js"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-green-400 hover:underline font-bold"
          >
            ethers
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-gray-400">(MIT)</span> — the read-only contract
          calls behind the mint module's live rate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-green-400 hover:underline font-bold"
          >
            Tailwind CSS
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-gray-400">(MIT)</span> — the styling, in the
          site's own theme.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/CloudCannon/pagefind"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-green-400 hover:underline font-bold"
          >
            Pagefind
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-gray-400">(MIT)</span> — indexes these pages into
          the site search at build time, no server involved.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/catdad/canvas-confetti"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-green-400 hover:underline font-bold"
          >
            canvas-confetti
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-gray-400">(ISC)</span> — the confetti bursts when
          you complete a module, vendored into the site so nothing loads from
          a CDN.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm mt-4">
        The trainer, the mint simulation, and the verification walk
        themselves are hand-built for this section — no game engine, no
        trackers, nothing loaded from a CDN.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
